WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.720
In late 1814, the great powers of Europe gathered
after the fall of Napoleon to discuss what

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:06.720
to do next.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:08.770
At the time, the continent was in ruins.

00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:14.870
The old order had been exploded as effectively
as if someone had detonated an atom bomb beneath

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:15.870
it.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:22.680
Just as it would in 1945, Europe needed a
new roadmap, a blueprint for the future.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.780
Designing that blueprint fell to one man:
Metternich.

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:31.190
The foreign minister of the Austrian Empire,
Metternich was a dandy, a womanizer, and a

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:33.449
comically pompous fop.

00:00:33.449 --> 00:00:36.819
He was also one of the greatest diplomats
who ever lived.

00:00:36.819 --> 00:00:42.030
Under his guidance, post-Napoleon Europe was
forged into a conservative system that would

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:44.420
last nearly 100 years.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:49.809
His blueprint ensured the continent’s 19th
Century was far less bloody than either it’s

00:00:49.809 --> 00:00:51.319
18th or 20th.

00:00:51.319 --> 00:00:55.989
Yet Metternich also tried to strangle the
forces of nationalism at birth and, in doing

00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:59.089
so, set the stage for his own destruction.

00:00:59.089 --> 00:01:08.509
A master strategist to some, a vain buffoon
to others, this is the life of Europe’s

00:01:08.509 --> 00:01:11.880
coachman.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:20.460
Children of the Revolution
The world Metternich was born into would be

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.950
unrecognizable to us today.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:27.369
The center of Europe was dominated not by
Germany, but by something known as the Holy

00:01:27.369 --> 00:01:32.270
Roman Empire, a millennia-old collection of
tiny states all joined together.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:36.380
It was in one of these tiny states on the
west bank of the Rhine that Metternich was

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.060
born, on May 15, 1773.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:44.070
Or, to give him his full name: Klemens Wenzel
Nepomuk Lothar, Fürst von Metternich-Winneburg-Beilstein.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:53.560
Although Metternich 
was from the Germanic Holy Roman Empire, it

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.970
was France that had the biggest impact on
his early life.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:02.159
Metternich’s home state was right on the
buffer zone with the France of Louis XVI,

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:06.659
so the boy grew up speaking better French
than he did German, despite his father being

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:08.100
an Austrian envoy.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.120
This wasn’t all that unusual.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:14.080
France exerted a powerful pull over continental
Europe in the 18th Century, and it seemed

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.870
totally natural that Metternich should be
sent off to the French city of Strasbourg

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.120
to complete his education in 1788.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:26.800
Unfortunately for the teenage Metternich,
France at the end of the 1780s was a ticking

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:27.800
timebomb.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:33.800
On July 14, 1789, years of political turmoil
in Paris exploded onto the streets.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:38.050
A mob stormed the Bastille armory, lynching
the garrison commander.

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:42.930
It was the opening salvo of the French Revolution.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:46.570
And it was about to consume the entire continent.

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:51.590
Despite being the sort of age when waving
the revolutionary flag might seem appealing,

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:54.270
Metternich was disgusted by the unfolding
drama.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:58.520
For Metternich, any upending of the natural,
conservative order of Europe was anathema.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:01.370
He decamped Strasbourg for Mainz post haste.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:05.930
But Metternich was about to learn a very important
lesson: when the cannonball of revolution

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.640
gets rolling, there’s no escape.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:14.230
In April, 1792, a French army whipped into
a revolutionary frenzy invaded the Holy Roman

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:17.550
Empire, burning, pillaging, destroying.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:22.250
By now posted to a diplomatic position in
the Austrian Netherlands (AKA Belgium) alongside

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:26.870
his father, Metternich was forced to watch
in horror as the French annexed his family’s

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:30.090
home state in 1794, leaving them with nothing.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:34.250
Barely had he got his head around this when
the French overran the Austrian Netherlands

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:37.470
and the boy and his father were forced to
flee to Vienna.

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:41.490
Although the Metternichs were now refugees,
they were still people of means, with good

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:42.690
connections to Austria’s elite.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:48.450
So Metternich’s exile was less ‘Moses
in the desert’ and more ‘rich playboy

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.030
sleeping around Vienna’.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:55.060
He even managed to marry Eleonore von Kaunitz,
the daughter of Austria’s former chancellor.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:59.630
While Metternich was serially unfaithful to
her, the marriage still meant his family going

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:01.800
up in the world of Austrian politics.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:05.710
But fate, and France, weren’t done with
Metternich.

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:10.820
On November 9, 1799, a French army officer
staged a coup in Paris, putting himself in

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.540
charge of the revolutionary war machine.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.470
His name was Napoleon Bonaparte.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:20.420
And he was about to plunge Europe into chaos.

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:28.760
The Napoleonic Era
From Metternich’s perspective, the 19th

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:34.010
Century must have felt like settling into
a comfy armchair that suddenly grows teeth

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:35.710
and devours you.

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:40.770
In 1801, Vienna’s newest man about town
was promoted to ambassador and sent first

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:43.320
to Dresden, and then to Berlin.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:48.100
As Napoleon kept conquering, Metternich enjoyed
the Prussian capital’s nightlife, all while

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:53.230
gamely trying to persuade his hosts to join
Vienna in war against France.

00:04:53.230 --> 00:05:01.510
For a guy known for his diplomatic acumen,
Metternich totally messed up this one.

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:07.890
After years of cajoling, the Prussians finally
put their foot down in 1805 and told the Austrian

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:08.890
fop “Nein!”

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:15.220
This was the moment the geopolitical armchair
swallowed the young ambassador whole.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:20.960
On December 2, 1806, the Russian, Holy Roman,
and Austrian armies – minus Prussia – all

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:24.260
met to battle France at Austerlitz.

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:28.760
The Napoleonic army mopped the floor with
them.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:35.500
The Third Coalition lost the battle so badly
the Holy Roman Empire was destroyed.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:38.690
Austria was forced to become allies with France.

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:41.780
Would things have gone different if Prussia
had been there?

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:45.450
It’s hard to say, but they couldn’t exactly
have gone worse.

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:50.820
Still, Metternich’s catastrophic fail in
Berlin didn’t stop him from getting an ambassadorship

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:54.310
to Paris under Austria’s now pro-Napoleon
leadership.

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:56.870
Sadly, Metternich hadn’t learned from his
mistakes.

00:05:56.870 --> 00:06:01.780
On May 2, 1808, an uprising in French-occupied
Spain triggered a crisis in Paris.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:06.740
Egged on by Metternich’s breathless reports
that Napoleon was losing his grip on power,

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:08.630
Vienna took a risky gamble.

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:12.610
In 1809, Austria rolled everything on a surprise
attack.

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:14.380
Care to guess how this one went?

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:19.590
The War of the Fifth Coalition was a monumental
embarrassment for Austria.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:21.520
Napoleon crushed them.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:26.260
Not only that, he imposed one of the most
humiliating peace treaties in history.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:32.050
Vienna lost a ton of territory, and was forced
to pay Paris a ton of money.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:35.060
For Metternich personally, though, things
turned out surprisingly well.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:38.180
In the aftermath of the new treaty, he escaped
blame.

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:40.160
Not only that, he got promoted.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:45.590
On October 8, 1809 Emperor Francis I made
Metternich, then just 36 years old, foreign

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:46.590
minister.

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:49.520
He would hold the post for 40 years.

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:53.600
With Napoleon now basically in charge of Europe,
Austria’s new foreign minister was forced

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:58.100
to use all his legendary charm to try and
placate Paris.

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:03.190
This created some humiliating moments, such
as when Napoleon invaded Russia in 1812.

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:06.490
Austria was forced to send its own army along
in support of the French.

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:11.340
While Metternich managed to secure their nominal
independence under Austrian commanders, it

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:14.130
was crumbs from Napoleon’s table.

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:15.740
Or so it seemed.

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:19.930
When Napoleon’s latest adventure unexpectedly
blew up in his face, Austria was able to sign

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:21.720
a peace deal with the Russians.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:26.080
Vienna could then sit out the catastrophic
French retreat across the continent.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:30.220
For his part, Metternich didn’t believe
this was really the end of Napoleon.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:35.320
In mid-1813, he tried to broker a peace between
France and Russia and Prussia that would allow

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:37.220
Napoleon to stay on the throne.

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:42.280
It was only when Napoleon said “non” that
Metternich signed Austria up to the Sixth

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:43.370
Coalition.

00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:47.790
If you recognize the name “the Sixth Coalition”,
it’s because these are the guys that actually

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:52.070
did it, the ones that rid Europe of its tyrant.

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:56.690
On March 31, 1814, the armies of the Sixth
Coalition captured Paris.

00:07:56.690 --> 00:08:00.690
With Cossacks marching down the banks of the
Seine, Napoleon finally threw in the towel.

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:04.770
The Petit Corporal abdicated and was exiled
to the island of Elba.

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:08.200
Across Europe, cries of victory went up.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:11.380
There was just one teeny, tiny problem.

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:14.590
What the heck did everyone do now?

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:22.040
The Congress of Vienna #1: Creating the Congress
It’s time for us to take a quick step back

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:25.510
in time to something called the Treaty of
Utrecht.

00:08:25.510 --> 00:08:30.389
Signed in 1713 at the end of the War of Spanish
Succession, the treaty was designed to keep

00:08:30.389 --> 00:08:35.569
Europe’s superpowers from superkilling each
other by delicately balancing power between

00:08:35.569 --> 00:08:36.569
them.

00:08:36.569 --> 00:08:39.479
The French Revolution had exposed the limits
of this century old treaty.

00:08:39.479 --> 00:08:44.059
Even before Napoleon’s defeat in 1814, everyone
knew a new treaty was needed, one that would

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:47.899
insure against another continent-wide war.

00:08:47.899 --> 00:08:51.680
Unfortunately, all the great powers that had
just defeated France all wanted different

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:52.680
things.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:54.250
We’ll get into the nitty gritty in a minute.

00:08:54.250 --> 00:09:00.839
Just know for now that everyone in 1814 was
aware a mishandled post-Napoleon peace could

00:09:00.839 --> 00:09:02.709
be catastrophic.

00:09:02.709 --> 00:09:07.920
Before the Sixth Coalition had even won, they’d
agreed to a congress to hammer all their differences

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:08.920
out.

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:12.149
Somehow, Metternich convinced them to hold
it in Vienna.

00:09:12.149 --> 00:09:17.639
The Congress of Vienna is one of the most
important diplomatic gatherings in history.

00:09:17.639 --> 00:09:21.959
So, to make sure we can all get our heads
around this extremely important event, we’re

00:09:21.959 --> 00:09:25.269
gonna take a quick detour now through the
main players.

00:09:25.269 --> 00:09:29.550
Ready for some hardcore learning, guys?!

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:36.040
The four key powers at the congress were Austria
(obviously), Russia, Prussia, and Great Britain.

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:40.480
Since Austria was playing on their home turf,
that meant putting Metternich in charge of

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:41.480
everything.

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:45.220
His goals were to maintain the balance of
power, by ensuring post-war France didn’t

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:49.290
get too weak, and Russia and Prussia didn’t
get too strong.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:54.480
Britain meanwhile sent over a guy called Viscount
Castlereagh, who broadly agreed with Metternich

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:59.529
about not destroying France, and not giving
Russia and Prussia everything.

00:09:59.529 --> 00:10:04.009
Prussia sent a delegation who were led from
behind the scenes by the big boss, Frederick

00:10:04.009 --> 00:10:05.120
Wilhelm III.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:10.529
Ol’ Freddie’s grand plan was to hobble
France and gobble up Saxony for Berlin.

00:10:10.529 --> 00:10:13.459
Finally, Russia sent the head honcho himself.

00:10:13.459 --> 00:10:17.290
Tsar Alexander I was the Congress’s wild
card.

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:22.550
Not only was he negotiating for Russia personally,
he also wanted some odd things like giving

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:28.069
the Poles their own country; a big deal since
Prussia and Austria both owned Polish territory.

00:10:28.069 --> 00:10:34.769
Although the table was designed for four people,
there was one last guy in the mix.

00:10:34.769 --> 00:10:40.050
Charles-Maurice de Talleyrand-Périgord, better
known as just Talleyrand, was a French diplomat

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:42.569
and weasel extraordinaire.

00:10:42.569 --> 00:10:48.300
He was so adaptable that he’d served under
Louis XVI, the French Revolutionary government,

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:49.300
and Napoleon.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:53.830
And now he was in Vienna, claiming to represent
the recently-restored French monarch, Louis

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:54.830
XVIII.

00:10:54.830 --> 00:10:58.759
Obviously, Prussia and Russia tried to laugh
Talleyrand out the room.

00:10:58.759 --> 00:11:00.339
France had just been defeated!

00:11:00.339 --> 00:11:02.660
There was no seat for him!

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:04.260
But Metternich thought differently.

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:08.870
Remember, he wanted to make France weaker
than under Napoleon, but not so weak that

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:11.870
it created a power vacuum Prussia might exploit.

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:17.819
So he teamed up with Castlereagh and got Talleyrand
into the table’s new fifth seat.

00:11:17.819 --> 00:11:21.870
Those are our players: Metternich for Austria,
Castlereagh for Britain, Frederick Wilhelm

00:11:21.870 --> 00:11:27.209
III for Prussia, Alexander I for Russia, and
Talleyrand for France.

00:11:27.209 --> 00:11:33.889
All of them wanted different things, and all
of them held the fate of Europe in their hands.

00:11:33.889 --> 00:11:38.050
As the entourages of these great men descended
on Vienna in the fall of 1814, the pendulum

00:11:38.050 --> 00:11:42.990
of history could have swung any one of a trillion
ways.

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:52.519
Luckily for the conservative faction, Metternich
had a plan.

00:11:52.519 --> 00:11:54.509
The Congress of Vienna #2: Parties and Politics
The words “Congress of Vienna” are probably

00:11:54.509 --> 00:11:58.819
conjuring images of boring dudes in silly
clothes sitting around tables and talking

00:11:58.819 --> 00:12:00.720
about impenetrable stuff.

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:03.610
Well, you couldn’t be more wrong.

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:08.610
The Congress of Vienna was designed by Metternich
to be the social event of the century.

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:11.199
There were parties almost every night.

00:12:11.199 --> 00:12:14.290
Glittering balls held by the glow of candlelight.

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:18.330
Excursions to the greatest salons imperial
Vienna had to offer.

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:19.559
The wine flowed freely.

00:12:19.559 --> 00:12:20.610
There was dancing.

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:21.689
Romantic liaisons.

00:12:21.689 --> 00:12:22.759
Drunken affairs.

00:12:22.759 --> 00:12:27.610
One source we’ve seen even claims two of
the top participants nearly got in a duel.

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:30.399
All of this debauchery wasn’t dereliction
of duty.

00:12:30.399 --> 00:12:32.550
It was part of Metternich’s plan.

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:37.379
Remember, before he was Austrian foreign minister,
Metternich was a womanizing party animal in

00:12:37.379 --> 00:12:38.379
Vienna.

00:12:38.379 --> 00:12:39.970
This was his territory.

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:44.261
He was the master of the quiet conversation
over cigars in some smoky club.

00:12:44.261 --> 00:12:46.839
Of a well placed word on the dancefloor.

00:12:46.839 --> 00:12:49.519
Of buttering up someone over wine.

00:12:49.519 --> 00:12:54.319
As the great men of history drank and danced
and seduced their way across the capital,

00:12:54.319 --> 00:12:57.629
Metternich was working the crowd like a Silicon
Valley hotshot.

00:12:57.629 --> 00:13:00.310
He had the perfect allies for his endeavor.

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:03.689
Talleyrand was even more of a social butterfly
than Metternich was.

00:13:03.689 --> 00:13:07.559
While they had their differences, the two
were able to deploy a joint charm offensive

00:13:07.559 --> 00:13:12.589
to make sure Russia and Prussia didn’t demand
France be carved up.

00:13:12.589 --> 00:13:13.970
Castlereagh was even better.

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:19.249
While he wasn’t a total bro, he was representing
Britain, the country with the deepest pockets

00:13:19.249 --> 00:13:20.249
in the world.

00:13:20.249 --> 00:13:25.389
And Castlereagh, who saw eye to eye with Metternich
on many major issues, wasn’t afraid to splash

00:13:25.389 --> 00:13:28.110
London’s cash to get what he wanted.

00:13:28.110 --> 00:13:32.149
By February, 1815, the final shape of the
congress was clearly visible.

00:13:32.149 --> 00:13:36.800
Alexander’s plan to liberate the Poles had
been bought off by offering him a chunk of

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:41.209
Poland, while Prussia had agreed to make do
with just three fifths of Saxony.

00:13:41.209 --> 00:13:46.569
France, meanwhile, was to be pushed back into
its pre-revolutionary borders, smaller but

00:13:46.569 --> 00:13:47.569
still powerful.

00:13:47.569 --> 00:13:52.029
The Holy Roman Empire would be replaced with
39 states collectively known as the German

00:13:52.029 --> 00:13:55.869
Confederation, with Austria as its head, naturally.

00:13:55.869 --> 00:13:59.509
There were other outcomes, too, including
a guarantee of Swiss neutrality, and a commitment

00:13:59.509 --> 00:14:03.649
to free trade along Europe’s rivers, a clause
cherished by Castlereagh.

00:14:03.649 --> 00:14:08.439
But the big, headline news was the establishment
of the congress system.

00:14:08.439 --> 00:14:12.420
Spearheaded by Metternich, the new system
would commit Europe’s great powers to meeting

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:16.490
every year or so to discuss events and (hopefully)
avoid war.

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:21.480
It also committed Europe’s powers to supporting
monarchs and empires over nationalist movements.

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:25.110
This was perhaps Metternich’s biggest contribution
to world history.

00:14:25.110 --> 00:14:29.430
Metternich deeply believed that most Europeans
were natural conservatives who preferred order

00:14:29.430 --> 00:14:32.279
and safety to the madness of revolution.

00:14:32.279 --> 00:14:34.450
And now everyone was agreeing with him.

00:14:34.450 --> 00:14:38.990
The Europe that emerged from the congress
wasn’t some shiny new thing, but a place

00:14:38.990 --> 00:14:42.249
governed by king, family, and God.

00:14:42.249 --> 00:14:47.439
It was everything Metternich had wanted, at
least in the broader sense.

00:14:47.439 --> 00:14:52.480
But, before he got it, there was one last
nightmare to live through.

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:56.940
On March 7, 1814, Metternich was woken by
an aide with some terrible news, not just

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:59.759
for Metternich, but the entire congress.

00:14:59.759 --> 00:15:01.369
Napoleon had escaped Elba.

00:15:01.369 --> 00:15:03.610
Not only that, he was on his way back to France.

00:15:03.610 --> 00:15:07.009
And the French people were rallying to his
cause.

00:15:07.009 --> 00:15:09.670
Such news could only mean one thing.

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:12.410
It was time for war again.

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:20.170
The Age of Metternich
When the news of Napoleon’s escape reached

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:21.939
the congress, panic hit.

00:15:21.939 --> 00:15:24.750
Within days, Metternich was making plans to
depart Vienna.

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:27.930
In France, the news caused the restored Louis
XVIII to flee the country.

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:32.429
It was like waking from a nightmare to find
you’re still dreaming.

00:15:32.429 --> 00:15:36.899
Europe was almost paralyzed, by the fear that
Napoleon’s return would be even worse than

00:15:36.899 --> 00:15:38.069
his first reign.

00:15:38.069 --> 00:15:40.410
Luckily, things didn’t quite work out like
that.

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:44.449
The period of Napoleon’s return is known
as the Hundred Days, because that’s roughly

00:15:44.449 --> 00:15:45.660
how long it lasted.

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:50.269
By June 18, 1815, the Seventh Coalition had
forced the Battle of Waterloo.

00:15:50.269 --> 00:15:54.250
There, the British and the Prussians defeated
the Petit Corporal once and for all.

00:15:54.250 --> 00:15:58.779
Back in Vienna, the Congress hadn’t actually
had time to disband by the time of Waterloo.

00:15:58.779 --> 00:16:00.199
Metternich himself was still in the city.

00:16:00.199 --> 00:16:05.120
Which turned out to be fortunate when Prussia
and Russia demanded France be made to pay

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:07.180
for this latest insult.

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:10.649
Metternich marshalled all his charm to make
sure the Congress’s outcome remained the

00:16:10.649 --> 00:16:12.269
same as in February.

00:16:12.269 --> 00:16:15.059
Remarkably, the Hundred Days changed almost
nothing.

00:16:15.059 --> 00:16:18.810
In July, 1815, the Congress of Vienna finally
disbanded.

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:23.160
With it went the era of revolution, the era
of Napoleon.

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:26.879
In its place came the age of Metternich.

00:16:26.879 --> 00:16:32.160
It was during this new age, as Europe was
cautiously settling into a life without war,

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:35.279
that Metternich became known as the Coachman
of Europe.

00:16:35.279 --> 00:16:38.009
Certainly, he was in the driver’s seat on
many occasions.

00:16:38.009 --> 00:16:42.379
In 1819, he headed the Carlsbad Conference
that committed the German Confederation to

00:16:42.379 --> 00:16:46.820
cracking down hard on freedom of speech, repressing
liberal-national movements, and monitoring

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:48.420
universities for sedition.

00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:51.499
He also headed a string of Great Power conferences.

00:16:51.499 --> 00:16:56.230
So successful were these that Francis I made
Metternich Chancellor of Austria.

00:16:56.230 --> 00:16:59.689
However, it wasn’t all roses for Europe’s
new driver.

00:16:59.689 --> 00:17:00.880
Metternich wasn’t an idiot.

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:05.679
He knew history was turning in favor of national
movements, and he hoped to nip them in the

00:17:05.679 --> 00:17:06.679
bud.

00:17:06.679 --> 00:17:10.120
Back home, he actually drew up a plan for
Austria’s many ethnic groups to get their

00:17:10.120 --> 00:17:11.120
own parliaments.

00:17:11.120 --> 00:17:16.449
He hoped giving the Hungarians, Czechs, Italians,
and so-on a shred of autonomy would inoculate

00:17:16.449 --> 00:17:18.740
Austria against further revolution.

00:17:18.740 --> 00:17:20.299
But Francis shot him down.

00:17:20.299 --> 00:17:25.240
The emperor was all like “dude, I loved
your secret police and crushing of free speech,

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:28.870
but your later stuff, with all that national
pride for Hungarians?

00:17:28.870 --> 00:17:30.649
Not a fan.”

00:17:30.649 --> 00:17:33.630
Still, Metternich remained a big figure on
the world stage.

00:17:33.630 --> 00:17:38.120
Even when his Congress system broke down over
an argument with Britain about the Greek revolution,

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:41.700
people clung to the basic ideas he’d outlined
in Vienna.

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:44.940
In fact, by the 1830s, they were clinging
harder than ever.

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:50.019
On July 27, 1830, a three day rebellion broke
out in France that overthrew the restored

00:17:50.019 --> 00:17:52.850
royals and replaced them with the July Monarchy.

00:17:52.850 --> 00:17:57.610
Barely a month later, Belgium exploded in
a national uprising against the Netherlands.

00:17:57.610 --> 00:18:01.450
Just three months after that, the Poles also
went into rebellion against their Russian

00:18:01.450 --> 00:18:02.450
overlords.

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:07.610
And then 1831 rolled around and an Italian
uprising forced the Austrians into armed intervention.

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:13.580
For those who’d started to doubt Metternich’s
system, the multiple revolutions of 1830 – 1831

00:18:13.580 --> 00:18:15.120
were a wakeup call.

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:19.789
The rulers of Europe clamped down hard on
nationalist feeling, which must have left

00:18:19.789 --> 00:18:22.419
Metternich feeling pretty vindicated.

00:18:22.419 --> 00:18:25.299
But the revolutions could be seen another
way, too.

00:18:25.299 --> 00:18:30.280
A sign that Metternich’s new system was
built not atop firm ground, but a rumbling

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:31.280
volcano.

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:36.590
It would only be a matter of time before that
volcano erupted.

00:18:36.590 --> 00:18:41.769
Decline and…

00:18:41.769 --> 00:18:45.600
Even as Metternich’s stature grew on the
world stage, he was starting to slip into

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:48.179
the twilight of his career at home.

00:18:48.179 --> 00:18:53.669
In the Viennese court, a dashing new liberal
Bohemian prince, Franz Anton, Graf von Kolowrat,

00:18:53.669 --> 00:18:59.529
was suddenly the rising star, making Metternich
look clumsy and slow and reactionary.

00:18:59.529 --> 00:19:04.669
Metternich’s vanity was so offended by this
that he started claiming every major policy

00:19:04.669 --> 00:19:08.610
initiative in Austria as his own, even the
bad ones.

00:19:08.610 --> 00:19:11.150
Not that Metternich made many good policies
himself.

00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:13.029
Take the issue of succession.

00:19:13.029 --> 00:19:17.970
Francis I’s eldest son Ferdinand was known
by everyone to be intellectually disabled

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:20.269
to the point of being unable to rule.

00:19:20.269 --> 00:19:24.190
Francis had daughters and he had other sons,
all of whom could have succeeded him, but

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:28.659
Metternich was such a stickler for old fashioned,
eldest son only succession that he actually

00:19:28.659 --> 00:19:32.190
talked Francis into not passing over Ferdinand.

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:37.630
And so it was that, on March 2, 1835, Ferdinand
I ascended to the Austrian throne.

00:19:37.630 --> 00:19:42.840
Anyone hoping for a reformist ruler instead
got a guy who simply couldn’t rule.

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:48.010
As the years dragged by, Metternich’s choices
went from annoying to actively embarrassing.

00:19:48.010 --> 00:19:52.260
In the late 1830s, he tried to revive his
congress system, but no other great powers

00:19:52.260 --> 00:19:53.799
wanted to get involved.

00:19:53.799 --> 00:19:57.361
Europe may have been shaped by Metternich’s
system, but it sure as heck didn’t want

00:19:57.361 --> 00:19:58.559
to be ruled by him.

00:19:58.559 --> 00:20:02.009
Not that all the things making Metternich
unpopular were his fault.

00:20:02.009 --> 00:20:04.100
The famine that gripped Europe in the 1840s,
for example.

00:20:04.100 --> 00:20:08.470
Or the financial panic that tipped parts of
the continent into recession.

00:20:08.470 --> 00:20:12.389
What was Metternich’s fault, though, was
the secret police network watching everyone

00:20:12.389 --> 00:20:13.389
at home.

00:20:13.389 --> 00:20:17.669
Arresting people for dissent, giving them
nowhere to air their legitimate grievances.

00:20:17.669 --> 00:20:19.970
Instead, people had to bottle everything up.

00:20:19.970 --> 00:20:21.360
Every pang of hunger.

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:22.400
Every miserable indignity.

00:20:22.400 --> 00:20:24.850
It was a recipe ripe for disaster.

00:20:24.850 --> 00:20:30.350
All it needed was an outside shock to send
everything crashing down.

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:34.190
In 1848, that shock finally came.

00:20:34.190 --> 00:20:42.080
No Country for One Old Man
If you study 19th Century European history

00:20:42.080 --> 00:20:47.070
– or, better yet, just watch our videos
on the subject – 1848 is the year you’ll

00:20:47.070 --> 00:20:52.019
keep coming back to, because that’s the
year that everything went bananas.

00:20:52.019 --> 00:20:53.940
It started on February 23.

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:58.130
That day a French government ban on holding
banquets triggered a revolution that toppled

00:20:58.130 --> 00:20:59.710
the July Monarchy.

00:20:59.710 --> 00:21:04.130
The crash with which the king fell was enough
to bring Metternich tumbling down too.

00:21:04.130 --> 00:21:06.789
By now, Metternich had been in power for nearly
forty years.

00:21:06.789 --> 00:21:10.850
He was a symbol of everything that was old
and cautious and conservative in Europe.

00:21:10.850 --> 00:21:16.039
So, when news of the latest French Revolution
reached Vienna, it made everyone hope Austria

00:21:16.039 --> 00:21:17.539
could change too.

00:21:17.539 --> 00:21:21.539
Within days of the July Monarchy’s collapse,
petitions were circulating through Vienna,

00:21:21.539 --> 00:21:22.820
demanding liberalization.

00:21:22.820 --> 00:21:25.110
These petitions weren’t calling for Metternich’s
head.

00:21:25.110 --> 00:21:27.059
They just wanted an end to his system.

00:21:27.059 --> 00:21:31.909
But since, under that system, the act of signing
a petition was illegal, Metternich demanded

00:21:31.909 --> 00:21:35.740
all the tens of thousands of people who’d
put their names down be arrested.

00:21:35.740 --> 00:21:39.429
You know that old phrase “to shoot yourself
through the foot?”

00:21:39.429 --> 00:21:43.340
This was Metternich accidentally blowing his
own head off.

00:21:43.340 --> 00:21:47.549
The short version of what happened next is
that Vienna’s poor, liberal students decided

00:21:47.549 --> 00:21:49.639
to protest the government’s crackdown.

00:21:49.639 --> 00:21:53.490
On March 13, 1848, a huge crowd gathered in
the city.

00:21:53.490 --> 00:21:55.350
Metternich demanded the crowd be crushed.

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:58.730
The government’s anti-Metternich faction
counselled peace, with the result that it

00:21:58.730 --> 00:22:01.470
was decided to maybe not crush the crowd,
but…

00:22:01.470 --> 00:22:02.470
I dunno.

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:03.470
Squash them?

00:22:03.470 --> 00:22:04.470
A little?

00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:08.090
Whatever the correct metaphor, the cavalry
were sent in at exactly the wrong strength.

00:22:08.090 --> 00:22:12.950
Wrong, because there weren’t enough of them
to disperse the crowd, or do anything but

00:22:12.950 --> 00:22:14.059
spark a panic.

00:22:14.059 --> 00:22:18.100
The panicked crowd attacked the cavalry, who
fought back, killing five students.

00:22:18.100 --> 00:22:22.350
The firefight triggered a mass insurrection
and the civil guard were called in.

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:26.179
Unfortunately, the civil guard took one look
at the unfolding revolution and decided to

00:22:26.179 --> 00:22:28.059
side with the revolutionaries.

00:22:28.059 --> 00:22:33.559
With the entire city in revolt, Metternich
was given until 9pm to resign.

00:22:33.559 --> 00:22:35.200
At first, Metternich refused.

00:22:35.200 --> 00:22:41.210
But, as the clock ticked down and the situation
got uglier, reality finally dawned on the

00:22:41.210 --> 00:22:42.610
aging Chancellor.

00:22:42.610 --> 00:22:45.060
Just before the stroke of nine, Metternich
announced his resignation.

00:22:45.060 --> 00:22:51.529
The very next day, March 14, 1848, he fled
into exile in London.

00:22:51.529 --> 00:22:53.159
The age of Metternich was over.

00:22:53.159 --> 00:22:55.260
The next two years were crazy.

00:22:55.260 --> 00:22:59.710
As Metternich watched impotently from England,
Ferdinand I was overthrown and replaced with

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:00.970
Emperor Franz Joseph.

00:23:00.970 --> 00:23:03.630
The Hungarians and Italians went into rebellion.

00:23:03.630 --> 00:23:05.549
Prussia’s liberals revolted.

00:23:05.549 --> 00:23:09.000
When the dust finally settled, though, one
thing was clear.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:13.620
Somehow, against all the odds, Metternich’s
system was still standing.

00:23:13.620 --> 00:23:16.720
Yep, the revolutions of 1848 basically failed.

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:19.230
The old order was restored across the continent.

00:23:19.230 --> 00:23:23.870
Even France simply replaced the July Monarchy
with the dictatorship of Napoleon III.

00:23:23.870 --> 00:23:27.210
In 1851, Metternich was even allowed to return
to Vienna.

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:32.210
It was a triumph, of sorts, and the old statesman
was soon back in the corridors of power.

00:23:32.210 --> 00:23:34.580
But time was no longer on his side.

00:23:34.580 --> 00:23:37.659
On June 11, 1859, Metternich’s health gave
out.

00:23:37.659 --> 00:23:40.850
He passed away in Vienna, aged 86.

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:44.610
But while Metternich the man was gone, the
system he created would stay in place for

00:23:44.610 --> 00:23:46.370
another 60 years.

00:23:46.370 --> 00:23:50.730
In various guises, Metternich’s blueprint
for Europe remained until WWI.

00:23:50.730 --> 00:23:53.860
It also succeeded in its mission to keep the
peace.

00:23:53.860 --> 00:23:58.639
Between the Congress of Vienna and WWI, the
biggest war fought in mainland Europe was

00:23:58.639 --> 00:24:00.019
the Franco-Prussian War.

00:24:00.019 --> 00:24:05.159
Compared to the Napoleonic wars, or WWI and
WWII, it was peanuts.

00:24:05.159 --> 00:24:08.580
For all his faults, Metternich was a man who
built something that lasted.

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:13.970
He may have been vain, repressive, and sometimes
actively stupid, but he was still the man

00:24:13.970 --> 00:24:16.110
who sat in Europe’s driving seat for decades.

00:24:16.110 --> 00:24:37.860
For better or for worse, our world would be
very different without him.

